+++ b/docs/self-hosting.md
@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+## Self-hosting
+
+Fizzy ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) configuration that puts the
+whole application on one Linux server: a single container runs the web server
+and the background jobs, backed by SQLite and files on disk, with
+[kamal-proxy](https://kamal-deploy.org/docs/proxy) terminating SSL in front of
+it. Fork the repository, fill in your hostname, your mail server, and your
+secrets, and run `bin/kamal setup` to have Fizzy serving at
+`https://your-host`.
+
+Everything you need to fill in lives in two files: `config/deploy.yml` and
+`.kamal/secrets`.
+
+
+### What you need
+
+* A Linux server running a recent kernel, reachable over SSH. Ports 80 and 443
+  must be open, and Kamal will install Docker on it for you if it isn't there
+  already.
+* Docker on your own machine, to build the image Kamal pushes.
+* A domain name whose DNS record points at that server. kamal-proxy requests a
+  Let's Encrypt certificate for it, so it has to be publicly resolvable.
+* An account with a container registry to push the image to, e.g.
+  [Docker Hub](https://hub.docker.com) or [ghcr.io](https://github.com/features/packages).
+* A mail server that Fizzy can send through. Sign-in happens by magic link, so
+  there is no way into a Fizzy without one.
+* Ruby on your own machine to run the commands below: `bin/setup` installs
+  everything Fizzy needs.
+
+
+### 1. Fill in the deploy configuration
+
+Open `config/deploy.yml` and replace every `your-*` placeholder:
+
+* `your-host`, everywhere it appears, is the hostname you're deploying to.
+* `your-registry-username/fizzy` is the image Kamal builds and pushes, along
+  with the registry credentials in the `registry` section. Add
+  `server: ghcr.io` (or another registry) if you don't use Docker Hub.
+* The `env: clear` section holds the settings that aren't secret: the mail
+  server to send through, the address that mail is sent from, and how much to
+  log.
+
+
+### 2. Fill in the secrets
+
+Kamal reads secrets from `.kamal/secrets`, which is not committed:
+
+```sh
+cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+```
+
+Generate the values, then paste them into the file:
+
+* `SECRET_KEY_BASE`: run `bin/rails secret`.
+* `SMTP_USER_NAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD`: the credentials for your mail server.
+* `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`: needed only for browser push
+  notifications. The example file shows how to generate a pair.
+* `REGISTRY_PASSWORD`: an access token for your container registry.
+
+
+### 3. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+That bootstraps the server with Docker, builds and pushes the image, pushes the
+secrets and environment, starts kamal-proxy, and deploys. The entry point
+prepares the database, so the SQLite schema is created on the first boot.
+
+From then on, `bin/kamal deploy` ships new changes, and `bin/kamal rollback` to
+go back a version.
+
+
+### What you get
+
+* **HTTPS** at `https://your-host`, with the certificate issued and renewed by
+  kamal-proxy. Fizzy is told to assume SSL and force it, so cookies are secure.
+* **Data that survives deploys.** The `fizzy_storage` Docker volume holds
+  `storage/` — the SQLite databases, uploaded files, and cache. It's mounted
+  into every new release, so deploys, rollbacks, and reboots keep your data.
+  Point it at a path that gets backed up if the data matters to you.
+* **Background jobs on the box.** Solid Queue runs in the web container's Puma
+  process, so notifications, webhooks, and recurring tasks are taken care of
+  without a second server. Its dashboard is at `https://your-host/admin/jobs`.
+  Only staff can open it, so mark yourself as one from `bin/kamal console` with
+  `Identity.find_by!(email_address: "you@example.com").update!(staff: true)`.
+* **One container to keep an eye on.** `bin/kamal logs -f` follows everything.
+
+
+### First run
+
+Sign up at `https://your-host/signup` with your email address, and follow the
+magic link that arrives from your mail server. The first account created is the
+one everyone else joins, either by invitation or by the join code on its
+settings page.
+
+If the magic link doesn't arrive, check `bin/kamal logs` for SMTP errors. Mail
+servers that expect SSL from the start, rather than STARTTLS, need
+`SMTP_TLS: true` and usually port 465.
+
+
+### Growing out of one box
+
+The defaults are for a single server, but they don't lock you in:
+
+* Move background jobs to their own servers with the `jobs` role sketched out in
+  `config/deploy.yml`, and set `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: false` so the web container
+  only serves requests.
+* Keep uploads outside the box by setting `ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE: s3` along
+  with the `S3_*` variables that `config/storage.oss.yml` reads.
+* Swap SQLite for MySQL with `DATABASE_ADAPTER: mysql` and the `MYSQL_*`
+  variables that `config/database.mysql.yml` reads.
+
+
+### Troubleshooting
+
+* **No certificate issued**: make sure the DNS record for `your-host` points at
+  the server and that ports 80 and 443 are open, then run `bin/kamal proxy logs`.
+* **Can't push the image**: check the registry credentials in `config/deploy.yml`
+  and `REGISTRY_PASSWORD` in `.kamal/secrets`.
+* **Deploy hangs on the health check**: the app isn't answering on `/up`, so
+  look for the reason in `bin/kamal app logs`.
diff --git a/test/deploy_config_test.rb b/test/deploy_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9367e91
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deploy_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+require "test_helper"
+
+class DeployConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  setup do
+    @config = YAML.safe_load(ERB.new(File.read(Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))).result, aliases: true)
+  end
+
+  test "deploys Fizzy to one box, with SSL terminated by the proxy" do
+    assert_equal "fizzy", @config.fetch("service")
+    assert_equal [ "your-host" ], @config.dig("servers", "web")
+    assert_equal true, @config.dig("proxy", "ssl")
+    assert_equal "your-host", @config.dig("proxy", "host")
+  end
+
+  test "keeps data and serves assets across deploys" do
+    assert_equal [ "fizzy_storage:/rails/storage" ], @config.fetch("volumes")
+    assert_equal "/rails/public/assets", @config.fetch("asset_path")
+  end
+
+  test "uploads to disk and background jobs run in the web container" do
+    clear = @config.dig("env", "clear")
+    assert_equal "local", clear.fetch("ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE")
+    assert_equal true, clear.fetch("SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA")
+  end
+
+  test "the secrets example covers every secret the deploy config asks for" do
+    example = File.read(Rails.root.join(".kamal/secrets.example"))
+
+    @config.dig("env", "secret").each do |secret|
+      assert_includes example, "#{secret}=", ".kamal/secrets.example is missing #{secret}"
+    end
+  end
+end
diff --git a/test/environments/production_test.rb b/test/environments/production_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a0e4f86
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/environments/production_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "json"
+require "open3"
+
+class ProductionEnvironmentTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  SCRIPT = <<~RUBY
+    config = Rails.application.config
+    puts JSON.generate(
+      delivery_method: config.action_mailer.delivery_method,
+      smtp_settings: config.action_mailer.smtp_settings,
+      mailer_url_options: config.action_mailer.default_url_options,
+      controller_url_options: config.action_controller.default_url_options
+    )
+  RUBY
+
+  test "the app host and the mail server come from the environment" do
+    settings = boot_with \
+      "APP_HOST" => "fizzy.example.com",
+      "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "smtp.example.com",
+      "SMTP_PORT" => "465",
+      "SMTP_DOMAIN" => "example.com",
+      "SMTP_USER_NAME" => "fizzy",
+      "SMTP_PASSWORD" => "secret",
+      "SMTP_TLS" => "true",
+      "SMTP_AUTHENTICATION" => "login"
+
+    assert_equal "smtp", settings.fetch("delivery_method")
+    assert_equal({ host: "fizzy.example.com", protocol: "https" }, settings.fetch("mailer_url_options").symbolize_keys)
+    assert_equal({ host: "fizzy.example.com", protocol: "https" }, settings.fetch("controller_url_options").symbolize_keys)
+
+    smtp_settings = settings.fetch("smtp_settings")
+    assert_equal "smtp.example.com", smtp_settings.fetch("address")
+    assert_equal 465, smtp_settings.fetch("port")
+    assert_equal "example.com", smtp_settings.fetch("domain")
+    assert_equal "fizzy", smtp_settings.fetch("user_name")
+    assert_equal "secret", smtp_settings.fetch("password")
+    assert_equal "login", smtp_settings.fetch("authentication")
+    assert_equal true, smtp_settings.fetch("tls")
+  end
+
+  test "without a mail server, Action Mailer keeps its own defaults" do
+    settings = boot_with "APP_HOST" => "fizzy.example.com"
+
+    assert_nil settings.fetch("delivery_method")
+    assert_nil settings.fetch("smtp_settings")
+    assert_equal({ host: "fizzy.example.com", protocol: "https" }, settings.fetch("mailer_url_options").symbolize_keys)
+  end
+
+  test "without SSL, absolute URLs are built with http" do
+    settings = boot_with "APP_HOST" => "fizzy.example.com", "DISABLE_SSL" => "true"
+
+    assert_equal({ host: "fizzy.example.com", protocol: "http" }, settings.fetch("mailer_url_options").symbolize_keys)
+  end
+
+  private
+    def boot_with(variables)
+      # Drop whatever mail settings this machine has, so the runner only sees the
+      # ones passed in.
+      environment = ENV.to_h.reject { |key, _| key.start_with?("SMTP_") }
+        .merge("RAILS_ENV" => "production", "SECRET_KEY_BASE" => "production-test-secret")
+        .merge(variables)
+
+      output, error, status = Open3.capture3(environment, Rails.root.join("bin/rails").to_s, "runner", SCRIPT)
+      assert status.success?, "Booting production failed:\n#{error}"
+
+      JSON.parse(output)
+    end
+end
